2.3.0 - bumped the version to 2.3.0
	fixed sqlrsh csv output - non-ascii bytes, newlines, carriage returns,
		tabs, and nulls were dropped, and fields containing them
		weren't quoted
	fixed sqlrsh csv output - nulls and empty strings were written
		identically, nulls are now written as an unquoted empty field
		and empty strings as a quoted empty field
	fixed sqlrsh csv output - the ===== divider row was written between
		the header row and the data, the divider is now written for
		the plain format only
	added sqlrsh getasnumber, noelapsed, nextresultset, and quiet commands
	added sqlrsh -headers, -divider, -stats, -lazyfetch, -txqueries, and
		-delimiter command line options, and made -getasnumber,
		-noelapsed, -nextresultset, and -quiet accept an on|off value
	fixed sqlrsh -script - a script that ran without an error still
		exited 1, because the end of the file was treated as a failure
	added distinct sqlrsh exit codes - 0 success, 1 usage error, 2 script
		file couldn't be opened, 3 connection or authentication
		failure, 4 query or command error - and sqlrsh now checks the
		connection at startup so connection failures get their own
		code
	fixed sqlrsh csv output - the stats block was written after the data,
		which broke csv by default, the stats are now written for the
		plain format only
	fixed sqlrsh csv output - the cache command's banner was written at
		the top of the stream, the banner is now written for the
		plain format only
	fixed sqlrsh csv output - the fields command wrote the column names
		without quoting or escaping them, the names now go through the
		csv field writer
	added sqlrsh json and jsonl output formats - json writes one compact
		document per command, with the column names and types, the
		rows, and the stats, and jsonl writes one object per line so
		it streams - a database null is the json null, utf-8 and
		every other byte pass through unchanged, and the scalar
		commands, printbinds, the fields command, and errors all
		emit json too
	fixed sqlrsh -format - an unrecognized format name quietly fell back
		to plain, it's a usage error now, and the format command and
		the -format option share one list of names
	added sqlrsh commands for the rest of the sqlrconnection api -
		usecatalog, currentcatalog, useschema, databaseisschema,
		databasefeature, getautocommit, intransaction,
		transactionmodel, defaulttransactionmodel,
		defaultisolationlevel, suspendsession, resumesession,
		connectionport, connectionsocket, connect timeout,
		getconnecttimeout, getresponsetimeout,
		bindvariabledelimiters, bindvariabledelimitersupported, and
		getdebug - each one honors all four output formats, and a
		missing or invalid argument is a failed command
	fixed sqlrsh json and jsonl output - the delimiter, autocommit,
		response timeout, translatedquery, and querytree commands
		wrote plain text no matter what the format was, they write
		json now
	added sqlrsh commands for the rest of the sqlrcursor result set and
		column metadata api - columninfo, columninfo on|off,
		columncase, suspendresultset, resultsetid, resumeresultset,
		resumecachedresultset, closeresultset, cacheoff,
		cachefilename, totalrows, firstrowindex, and endofresultset -
		columninfo writes all thirteen pieces of metadata per column,
		as a labelled block in plain, a header row and a row per
		column in csv, and a list of objects with real booleans in
		json and jsonl, and the two resume commands write the result
		set they fetched, the way the opencache command does
	added sqlrsh commands for the rest of the sqlrcursor bind variable
		api - inputbindclob, inputbind with an explicit length,
		outputbind blob, outputbind clob, outputbind cursor,
		fetchfrombindcursor, countbindvariables, validatebinds,
		validbind, substitution, clearsubstitutions, filequery, and
		preparefilequery - each one honors all four output formats,
		and a missing or invalid argument is a failed command
	fixed sqlrsh printinputbind, printoutputbind, and
		printinputoutputbind - all three were recognized as commands
		but had no implementation at all, so they printed nothing and
		failed
	fixed sqlrsh bind variable values - blob values were written up to
		their first null byte, and blob values, the bind variable name
		when a bind was redefined, the type and its parameters, and a
		value whose type wasn't recognized were all leaked
	fixed sqlrsh inputbind - a value consisting of a single quote
		character wrote off the end of a heap buffer
	fixed sqlrsh outputbind and inputoutputbind usage messages - neither
		listed the forms the command actually takes, and
		inputoutputbind printed nothing at all for one of its errors
	fixed sqlrsh inputbind, inputbindblob, outputbind, and
		inputoutputbind - the command word on its own was sent to the
		database and came back as a syntax error instead of the
		command's usage
	updated the sqlrsh usage text, -help output, help command, and man
		page to cover every command and every command line option -
		about 109 command forms and 9 options were missing, including
		everything added in this release, and the four output formats,
		the csv quoting and null rules, and the exit codes are all
		written down now
	cleaned up source code comments added since the 2.1.1 release -
		de-versed narration back to step labels, trimmed
		over-specified rationale, removed summaries from above
		function definitions, and fixed typos, a few comments that
		contradicted the code, and some stray non-ascii characters
	the website's server-side includes are expanded at build time now
		instead of by apache - the 95 pages that use them are
		.html.in files, and the new ssi.sh and ssi.vbs scripts
		expand them into .html, so the site can be served by any
		web server, on unix or windows, and doc/.htaccess is gone
	fixed 19 stale api documentation links in the website's .wt sources -
		they'd been corrected in the generated html but never carried
		back to the sources, so rebuilding the docs reverted them
	the website's html files aren't executable anymore - the execute bit
		was only there for apache's XBitHack, which went away with
		the server-side includes, so they're installed 644 now like
		the css and js already were, and a few stray executable
		images were fixed while we were in there
	removed insertparts.pl - the doc build replaced it with the wt tool in
		2016 and nothing has referenced it since
	added a README.md, for the github mirror at
		github.com/firstworks-software/sqlrelay, which otherwise shows
		a bare file listing as its front page
	fixed a couple of memory leaks in sqlrsh - the default password taken
		from the config file and the history file name were both
		allocated and never freed
	fixed an out-of-bounds read in the sqlrsh nullsasnulls command - the
		command name was matched at 12 characters but the pointer was
		advanced 13, so a bare "nullsasnulls;" read past the end of
		the string
	fixed the sqlrsh -id tlsciphers default - the guard tested getValue()
		rather than isFound(), and getValue() returns an empty string
		rather than a null for an option that wasn't given, so the
		cipher list in the config file was never applied
	fixed sqlrsh command line options that take a value - a missing value
		came out empty, which meant 0 for the numeric options, so a
		trailing -resultsetbuffersize quietly set the buffer size to 0
		rather than being a usage error, and every other value-taking
		option was quietly dropped
	fixed the sqlrsh show only commands - only the twelve forms with a
		mysql, odbc or jdbc list format suffix were recognized, so a
		bare "show only tables;" and its views, aliases and synonyms
		twins fell through to the database as sql and failed, the
		bare forms now work and use the default list format, like
		"show tables" does
	fixed the sqlrsh clearinputbind, clearoutputbind and
		clearinputoutputbind commands - the variable name the help text
		has always shown was ignored and the whole list was cleared, a
		name now clears just that variable, no name still clears the
		whole list, and a name that is not in the list is a failed
		command
	fixed make install - it failed in doc/ on a host without the wt tool,
		because installing the website built it first and the build
		starts by running wt, the generated .html files are in version
		control again and install just copies them, so regenerating
		the site is a manual make in doc/, and make clean there now
		removes everything the makefiles generate
	fixed the sqlrsh connect timeout and response timeout commands - the
		part after the dot was echoed at four digits and only its
		first four characters were kept, so 5.000025 set no
		microseconds at all, and it went to the api as a whole number
		of microseconds while being documented as a fraction of a
		second, so "connect timeout 5.25" claimed 5.25 seconds and
		really set 5.0025, the part after the dot is now read as a
		decimal fraction of a second, which is what the c++ api and
		the SQLR_CLIENT_CONNECT_TIMEOUT and
		SQLR_CLIENT_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT environment variables have
		always meant by it, echoed padded to 6 places the way
		getconnecttimeout and getresponsetimeout already report it,
		and documented that way, so 5.25 sets and reports five and a
		quarter seconds, and both commands now reject a timeout that
		isn't a number or is out of range instead of quietly setting
		0 or wrapping around to a negative, which turned the timeout
		off while claiming to set it
	added the sqlrsh -continueonerror option and continueonerror command,
		which make a script or a -command list run every statement
		rather than stopping at the first one that failed, and which
		write the number of failed statements to stderr at the end of
		the run, and redefined exit code 4 as "at least one statement
		failed", since it no longer means that sqlrsh stopped there
	fixed make install - the tds protocol module was built but never
		installed, because nothing referenced the
		installsharedlib-tds target in src/protocols/Makefile, so
		protocol="tds" couldn't be used from a normal install
	added the tds_connectstrings auth module - an instance with no auths
		and no users configured falls back to an auth module named
		after each of its protocols, and tds_connectstrings didn't
		exist, so every login through the tds protocol module failed
	fixed the tds protocol module - a failed login left the client hanging,
		because sending the response packet overwrote the login
		failure, so the session ran on rather than ending
	fixed the tds protocol module - an unrecognized database version fell
		back to tds 4.2, which hung the client, since the width of
		some fields varies with the tds version, an unrecognized
		version now uses the version that the client asked for, and
		the versions that odbc and freetds report for sql server 2016
		through 2022 are recognized now
	fixed the tds protocol module - after a failed login, the done token
		reported success rather than an error, and the error token
		reported line 1 rather than line 0
	added the sqlrsh -final, -autocommit, -nullsasnulls, -debug,
		-validatebinds and -bindvariabledelimiters options, so that
		every setting that means something before the first query has
		run is reachable as a command line option as well as a
		command, and left cache, opencache and the per-query bind
		commands as commands only, since a cache file belongs next to
		the query it caches and a bind belongs to one query
	fixed the sqlrsh lazyfetch, txqueries and continueonerror commands - a
		bare one of them was matched with a trailing space, so it fell
		through to the database as a query rather than turning the
		setting off
	fixed the sqlrsh nullsasnulls command - a bare one, or one with an
		unrecognized value, did nothing at all, rather than turning
		the setting off the way every other on|off command does
	added the sqlrsh -fieldsas option and fieldsas command, which choose
		which getFieldAs method every field of a result set is fetched
		with - raw, number, boolean or date - so that
		getFieldAsBoolean() and getFieldAsDate(), neither of which
		sqlrsh could reach before, are reachable now, made getasnumber
		an alias for the number and raw modes so that -getasnumber and
		the getasnumber command still behave exactly as documented,
		guarded each mode so that a column the conversion means
		nothing for is written unchanged rather than turned into a
		number, a true or a date, wrote a converted date as
		[-]YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS[.uuuuuu] carrying only the parts the
		field had, and made json and jsonl type a converted field, so
		number gives a bare json number and boolean a bare json true
		or false
	fixed the tds protocol module - a query like "select 1" sent a row
		value length of 10 with no value bytes after it, because the
		odbc backend reports a column size of 10 for an int and intn
		only allows 1, 2, 4 or 8, so the client read the done token as
		row data and reported "Datastream processing out of sync" -
		bitn, fltn, moneyn and datetimn had the same problem
	fixed the tds protocol module - the notify id and service broker
		deployment lengths in a query notification header are byte
		counts, but they were read as character counts, which ran past
		the end of the request buffer and desynced the rest of the
		stream, and a header whose size was 0 looped forever
	fixed the tds protocol module - nchar, nvarchar, ntext and xml row
		values were sent as 8-bit data with a character count, they
		are sent as ucs-2 with a byte count now
	fixed the tds protocol module - nchar and nvarchar bind values were
		handed to the database as ucs-2 bytes, so N'foo' arrived as
		"f\0o\0o\0", they are converted now, and copied into the bind
		pool rather than pointing into the request buffer
	fixed the tds protocol module - the maximum length that column
		metadata reports for an nchar, nvarchar, ntext or xml column
		has to be in bytes, but the backend reports it in characters
	fixed the tds protocol module - a response packet's size was computed
		in 16 bits, so a negotiated packet size of 65536 truncated to
		0 and the send loop wrote empty packets forever, the
		negotiated size is capped at 65535 minus the 8-byte header now
	fixed the tds protocol module - the info and error token size was
		computed in 16 bits, so a message over about 32k characters
		overflowed it, the message is truncated to fit now
	fixed the tds protocol module - the pre-login response copied from a
		null pointer when the client sent no instance name option
	fixed the tds protocol module - the sspi buffer size test compared a
		variable against itself rather than against the size the
		client sent
	fixed the tds protocol module - a sql batch's size was computed in 16
		bits, so a batch over about 128k characters was truncated, and
		maxquerysize was read from the configuration and never
		enforced
	renamed the misleading size/length variables in the tds protocol
		module, and renamed the parameter of the ucs2 read() and
		write() methods in sqlrprotocol from "size" to "length", since
		it is a count of characters rather than of bytes
	added an sqlrsh reference to the website - the two synopsis lines on
		the command line programs page were stale and the only other
		thing that page said about sqlrsh was to run it and type
		help;, so there is a new sqlrsh reference page covering every
		command line option, every command in the groups the built-in
		help uses, the four output formats with a sample of each, the
		exit codes and the rc file, the command line programs page has
		the real synopsis lines and a link to the new page now, and it
		no longer claims that sqlrsh reads a system rc file, which it
		never did - it reads .sqlrshrc in the user's home directory
		and nothing else
	fixed the tds protocol module - every request packet's data buffer was
		allocated and never freed, so a session leaked memory in
		proportion to the number of requests it handled, packets are
		read into a reused memory pool now
	fixed the tds protocol module - the send loop never advanced past the
		data it had just sent, so a response larger than the
		negotiated packet size sent its first chunk over and over,
		and the data in each packet is sized from the negotiated
		packet size minus the 8-byte header now, rather than being
		the whole negotiated size
	fixed the tds protocol module - the pre-login, login, attention, sspi,
		federated authentication and transaction manager requests
		each took a pooled cursor that they had no use for
	fixed the tds protocol module - a request that arrived when no cursor
		was available killed the session without telling the client,
		an error is sent now
	fixed the tds protocol module - the client session exit status was
		always "error", so the log said nothing useful about how a
		session ended
	fixed the tds protocol module - a malformed remote procedure call's
		error token was appended to whatever was already in the
		response buffer and then never sent, so the client hung
	fixed the tds protocol module - char and varchar bind values pointed
		into the request buffer rather than being copied into the
		bind pool, and a null value's 0xFFFF length was taken as a
		real length
	fixed the tds protocol module - about 35 response fields were
		converted to little-endian twice, which is correct on a
		little-endian host and wrong on a big-endian one
	fixed the tds protocol module - the sql collation environment change
		and the column metadata flags were sent big-endian, so the
		client read a garbage collation and never saw a column's
		nullable, updateable or identity flags
	added tdsversion, packetsize and maxpacketsize parameters to the tds
		protocol module - tdsversion overrides the version that the
		module guesses from the backend's version string, for
		backends whose version string it can't work it out from
	fixed the tds protocol module - the login extension and change
		password blobs were gated on flags that weren't decoded until
		about 100 lines later, so they could never be read
	fixed the tds protocol module - the server name and version were read
		once when the module was created, so they went stale when the
		backend reconnected
	fixed the tds protocol module - the array of bind variable names was
		never freed
	fixed the tds protocol module - the tds 7.4 column metadata flags were
		gated on a version test against 74 rather than 740, so they
		were always sent, and three of them were shifted past the end
		of the 16-bit field
	fixed the tds protocol module - a date column's value added up the
		days in the preceding months with an assignment where an
		addition was meant, so it was wrong for every date, and it
		was sent as 2 bytes rather than the 3 the protocol calls for
	fixed the tds protocol module - a time column's value was always
		midnight, and datetime2 and datetimeoffset values were sent
		as a time and a date each carrying its own size, rather than
		as one value under one size
	fixed the tds protocol module - a column type reported by the backend
		indexed the type map with no bounds check, and the map was
		one entry short, so an informix lvarchar column read past the
		end of it
	fixed the tds protocol module - a system error was passed to the debug
		output as a printf format string
	cleaned up the tds protocol module - the info and error token builders
		are named for what they do, there are error senders that
		build and send in one call, init() and free() actually
		initialize and release the session state, and the two
		commented-out column encryption blocks are comments now
	fixed sqlrsh -id - the password encryption module named by the
		instance's passwordencryptionid was used without checking
		that it was found, so a missing or misnamed module
		segfaulted, and a one-way encryption silently produced a
		null password and a misleading authentication error -
		both are reported by name now and exit 1
	fixed the sqlr-import and sqlr-export -id tlsciphers default - the
		guard tested getValue() rather than isFound(), and getValue()
		returns an empty string rather than a null for an option that
		wasn't given, so the cipher list in the config file was never
		applied
	fixed sqlr-import and sqlr-export command line options that take a
		value - a missing value came out empty, which meant 0 for the
		numeric options, so a trailing -port quietly tried to connect
		to port 0 rather than being a usage error, and every other
		value-taking option was quietly dropped
	fixed sqlr-export -krbservice - the value was read from the -krb
		option rather than the -krbservice option, so the service name
		given was ignored and the built-in default was used instead
	moved the command line value-taking option check into sqlrcmdline, as
		missingValueOption(), which knows the connection options that
		every client tool takes, so sqlr-import and sqlr-export only
		list their own
	fixed sqlrsh show catalogs - the four show catalogs commands compared
		one byte too many, which dragged the literal's terminating null
		into the comparison, so only the bare forms matched and
		show catalogs like 'pattern', and its mysql, odbc and jdbc
		variants, fell through and were sent to the database as SQL,
		failing with a syntax error
	moved sqlrsh onto sqlrcmdline's missingValueOption() - it kept a
		private copy of the same check, with a list that repeated
		the 18 connection options sqlrcmdline already knows, so the
		two lists could drift and a value-taking option added to one
		would have been missed by the other
	added rpc support to the tds protocol module - sp_executesql,
		sp_prepare, sp_execute, sp_prepexec, sp_prepexecrpc,
		sp_unprepare, sp_cursoropen, sp_cursorprepare,
		sp_cursorexecute, sp_cursorprepexec, sp_cursorunprepare,
		sp_cursorfetch, sp_cursoroption and sp_cursorclose were all
		empty stubs that answered nothing, so a client that prepared
		a statement or opened a server-side cursor - which is what
		odbc and jdbc normally do - just hung, and each proc can now
		be sent either by numeric proc id or by name, the way freetds
		sends sp_execute
	fixed the tds protocol module's rpc option flags - fNoMetaData and
		fReuseMetaData were read from bits 2 and 3 rather than 1 and
		2, so the no-metadata request that freetds sends on
		sp_cursorfetch was read as something else
	fixed the tds protocol module's rpc parameters - they were never
		parsed at all on the numeric proc id path, the proc name was
		used after it was freed, and a named procedure's result set
		was never sent, so even exec someproc that selects returned
		nothing
	fixed the tds protocol module's rpc parameter collation - the five
		collation bytes were read for char and text parameters
		whatever the protocol version, but there is no collation
		before tds 7.1, so every parameter after the first one on a
		7.0 connection was read from the wrong offset, which read
		past the end of the request packet
	added text, ntext and xml rpc parameter values to the tds protocol
		module - they were skipped, and that is how freetds sends the
		statement and parameter declaration strings that sp_prepare
		and sp_executesql take
	added return values to the tds protocol module - returnValue() wrote
		a token byte and nothing else, so the prepared statement
		handle and cursor id that a client reads out of the first
		return value never arrived
	fixed the tds protocol module's attention signal packet type - it was
		0x05 rather than the 0x06 that MS-TDS 2.2.3.1.1 gives, so
		every client cancel was rejected as an invalid packet type
		and ended the session, and an attention is now acknowledged
		with a done that has the attention bit set
	fixed the tds protocol module's bind variable count - a sql batch
		left whatever bind count the last rpc on that cursor had set,
		so the next batch was sent to the database with bind values
		it had no bind variables for
	fixed the tds protocol module's bulk load - it returned false, which
		ended the session, so the client saw a dropped connection
		rather than an error, it now reports that bulk load isn't
		supported and keeps the session
	fixed sqlr-import segfault when -table wasn't given and the file name
		didn't end in the importer's extension, which included the
		.tbl and .seq examples in its own help text and man page -
		the extension is now removed only if the file name has it,
		matched case-insensitively like the importer choice, and a
		file name that leaves no name behind, like .xml, is a
		reported error rather than undefined behavior
	fixed sqlr-import -table being silently ignored for xml imports - the
		name recorded in the file always won, so rows landed in a table
		the user never named, and exit status was 0 either way, while
		csv honored -table, an explicitly set name now wins over any
		name derived from the import file, which is what the c++ api
		already documented, and -table is now documented in the help
		text, the man page, and the admin docs, which had documented
		the opposite
	fixed an out-of-bounds read in the mysql protocol module - its type
		map was one entry shorter than the datatype list it's indexed
		by, so an informix LVARCHAR column read past the end of the
		array and reported whatever byte followed it as the column
		type
	fixed the c++ api sqlrconnection::getDatabaseFeature() - an unknown
		feature name was printed to stdout even with debug off,
		corrupting the calling program's output, and the message was
		also missing its trailing newline
	fixed sqlrimportxml and sqlrimportcsv - importData() segfaulted when
		setFileName(NULL) was called and an object name was set,
		neither importer can read standard input, so a NULL or empty
		file name is now a reported error, and the c++ api docs no
		longer describe import from standard input as though it works
	fixed the odbc connection module - the size reported for wchar and
		wvarchar fields counted the utf-8 null terminator, so every
		wide value came back one byte longer than it really was, and
		a maxfieldsize smaller than the terminator underflowed the
		size
	fixed the c++ api's parse of the SQLR_CLIENT_CONNECT_TIMEOUT and
		SQLR_CLIENT_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT environment variables - the
		fraction of a second was truncated rather than rounded, so
		5.1 gave 5 seconds and 99999 microseconds instead of 100000,
		and the timeout never read back as what was asked for, and
		documented what format those variables take, which had never
		been written down anywhere
	fixed csv export in the c++ api - fields with embedded nulls were
		truncated, and fields from numeric columns weren't quoted,
		even when they contained a comma, quote, newline, or
		carriage return
	fixed the tds protocol module - the transaction descriptor header's
		two fields were read in the opposite order from the one
		ms-tds defines, the 8 byte transaction descriptor is read
		before the 4 byte outstanding request count now
	fixed the freetds connection module - varchar, nchar, and nvarchar
		columns were all reported as char, because ctlib collapses
		them into CS_CHAR_TYPE, the sybase usertype is used to tell
		them apart now, ms sql server sends no usertype at all so
		they still can't be told apart there
	fixed the tds protocol module - the done-in-proc token was sent
		without DONE_MORE, which sql server always sets, so freetds
		took it for the last done in the response, stopped reading
		there, and left the return status, return values, and
		done-proc in its buffer, putting every command after it one
		response behind and losing the column metadata that
		sp_prepare returns
	fixed the freetds connection module - varbinary columns were reported
		as binary, because ctlib collapses both into
		CS_BINARY_TYPE, the sybase usertype is used to tell them
		apart now, ms sql server sends no usertype at all so they
		still can't be told apart there
	fixed the freetds connection module - character column sizes were
		reported multiplied by the maximum bytes-per-character of
		the client charset, 4 times too large under utf-8, the
		login ping asks for a 1-character column now and uses the
		size that comes back to scale them down, since ctlib
		provides no way to find out what the client charset is
	made the tds protocol module warn when a listener is configured with
		tls="yes" - the module doesn't support tls, and used to
		accept the attribute and silently ignore it
	fixed the freetds connection module - date, time, datetime2 and
		datetimeoffset columns were all reported as unknown, which
		the tds protocol module sent as void with no data, they are
		reported as date, time, timestamp and datetimeoffset now,
		freetds prior to 1.4 sends no usertype for datetime2 or
		datetimeoffset so they are both reported as timestamp there
	implemented bulk load in the tds protocol module - the "insert bulk"
		statement that opens one is answered by the module instead
		of being passed to the backend, and the bulk load packets
		that follow it are read and loaded a row at a time
	fixed the tds protocol module - a long varchar column was described
		as varbinary, so a text column came back as binary data and
		couldn't be bulk loaded at all
	added setTranslateBindVariablesForThisQuery() and
		getTranslateBindVariablesForThisQuery() to sqlrservercontroller
		and sqlrservercursor, so a protocol module can run a single
		query without bind variable translation, even when the
		instance's translatebindvariables attribute is "yes"
	fixed the tds protocol module - with translatebindvariables on, a
		sql batch containing @-prefixed names had them rewritten as
		bind variables and was then run with no bind values, so
		"create procedure someproc @a int as ..." failed
	documented that the freetds connection reports column types and sizes
		less accurately than the odbc connection does against ms sql
		server, and that the odbc connection is the better choice
		when an application needs accurate column metadata - the
		freetds odbc driver works as well as the microsoft one, so
		that needs no vendor software
	fixed the sap connection module - varchar, nchar, and nvarchar columns
		were all reported as char, because ctlib collapses them into
		CS_CHAR_TYPE, the sybase usertype is used to tell them apart
		now
	fixed the tds protocol module - a long varbinary column was described
		as varbinary, so an image column was reported as binary
		rather than image
	fixed the freetds connection module - sybase unichar and univarchar
		columns were reported as text, because ctlib collapses them
		into CS_TEXT_TYPE, the sybase usertype is used to tell them
		apart now and they're reported as nchar and nvarchar
	fixed the tds protocol module - a decimal or numeric column's column
		metadata size was the column's precision rather than the
		widest the value can be on the wire, so a decimal(5,2) column
		was described with a size of 5 instead of 17
	fixed the tds protocol module - an rpc parameter declared char or nchar
		was passed along at the length that came over the wire rather
		than blank padded out to its declared size, so a char(20)
		parameter carrying "one" was bound as 3 characters where a
		real sql server would have padded it to 20
	fixed the tds protocol module - date, time, datetime2 and
		datetimeoffset columns were sent to a client that negotiated
		a tds version older than 7.3, which is where those types were
		introduced, a real sql server converts them to strings
		server-side and sends nvarchar in the iso/odbc rendering
		instead and the module does that now
	fixed the tds protocol module - a date-only or time-only string left
		the components it didn't supply at -1 and they were fed
		straight into the writers' unsigned arithmetic, so a date
		column went out with -1098300 three-hundredths of a second
	fixed the tds protocol module - datetime::parse's return value was
		ignored, so a string it couldn't parse was written from
		whatever it happened to leave behind
	fixed the tds protocol module - a datetimeoffset string's trailing
		"+hh:mm" was read as a third date/time part, which made the
		whole string unparseable, it's split off and parsed now
	fixed the tds protocol module - a datetimeoffset was written with a
		hardcoded zero timezone offset and with the local wall time
		rather than the utc instant, both are written correctly now
	fixed the odbc connection module - a SQL_TYPE_DATE column was
		reported as a datetime rather than a date
	fixed the tds protocol module - a stored procedure call with an
		output parameter appended the t-sql "output" keyword after
		the parameter's bind marker, and sql server rejects that
		outright, the direction is already carried by the bind
		itself - SQL_PARAM_OUTPUT in the odbc module and CS_RETURN in
		the freetds and sap ones
	fixed the firebird protocol module - the database parameter buffer
		walk left the length and value of any item it had no use
		for in the buffer, so a single unhandled item threw the
		walk out of step, and its loop test compared pointers for
		inequality rather than counting the bytes left, so an
		out-of-step walk stepped over the end of the buffer and
		spun forever - every item is framed and skipped by its
		length now, the walk is bounded, and the dpb version byte
		selects the item encoding
	fixed the tds protocol module - an sp_executesql or sp_cursoropen
		that supplied no bind values still had its statement run
		through bind variable translation, so a single-@ local
		variable or parameter declaration in it was rewritten as a
		bind marker and then run with nothing bound, the same defect
		that was fixed for a plain sql batch
	fixed the tds protocol module - a stored procedure's output
		parameter was sent back without its name, and as an 8 byte
		integer whatever width the client declared, so a client that
		read the name got an empty one and ct_describe reported
		CS_LONG_TYPE for what was sent as a CS_INT_TYPE
	fixed the tds protocol module - a stored procedure's return value was
		never sent, the RETURNSTATUS token carried a hardcoded 0
		where a real sql server sends what the procedure returned -
		a named procedure is called through odbc call syntax now,
		which gives the return value a bind of its own
	fixed the firebird protocol module - every status vector element in
		an op_response was written 64 bits wide rather than 32, the
		low word of the response's blob id was never written, and no
		vector was terminated with isc_arg_end, three faults that
		cancelled each other out and left the attach and transaction
		responses correct by accident - the elements are 32 bits
		now, the whole blob id is written, and every vector ends
		with isc_arg_end
	fixed the firebird protocol module - a buffer written into a
		response was never padded to a 4-byte boundary, and the info
		database response made up for it with a hand-written trailer
		that was only correct when the response buffer length was a
		multiple of 4 plus 3 - buffers are padded now, and that
		response is built like every other one
	fixed the firebird protocol module - the op code, arch, protocol
		version, ptype, dpb, tpb, and info item tables didn't match
		firebird's own headers, PROTOCOL_VERSION2 didn't exist at all,
		and 15 was called isc_arg_mach where firebird calls it
		isc_arg_next_mach - the tables match firebird now, and every
		debug decoder covers all of them
	added the firebird protocol module's missing constant families -
		CONNECT_VERSION, the CNCT_ user id tags, ptype_MASK and the
		pflag_ bits, the dpb sub-namespaces, isc_info_tra_,
		isc_info_blob_, isc_bpb_, fb_cancel_, the isc_info_sql_stmt_
		statement types, P_FETCH, and the object handle, statement
		flag, and cursor flag constants
	added firebird protocol module debug output for the connect version
		and for the tags inside the connect block's user id, both of
		which were read and then thrown away
	added the op codes firebird defines to the firebird protocol module's
		dispatch switch, so one it knows about but hasn't implemented
		is distinguishable from one nobody defines
	fixed the firebird protocol module's connect handshake - it answered
		every client with a hardcoded PROTOCOL_VERSION10, arch_generic,
		ptype_rpc accept and threw away the protocol list the client
		offered - the offered tuples are negotiated now, the way
		firebird's own accept_connection() does it, capped at
		PROTOCOL_VERSION12 and ptype_batch_send, which is as far as the
		module can speak until it can drive the protocol 13 and up
		authentication handshake
	added an op_reject response to the firebird protocol module's connect
		handshake, so a client that offers no protocol the module can
		speak gets a rejection it can report rather than an accept it
		can't use
	fixed the odbc connection module - a stored procedure's output
		parameters and return status were read before the result sets
		were drained, so a procedure that returned rows as well as an
		output parameter handed back the bind's initial value rather
		than what the procedure set - the rows are buffered now, and
		the result sets drained, before the output binds are read
	fixed the php pdo connecttime option - the fraction of a second was
		truncated rather than rounded, so connecttime=5.1 set 5
		seconds and 99999 microseconds and never read back as what
		was asked for, the same defect the c++ api had before this
		release, and a fraction that rounds up now carries into the
		seconds without wrapping them negative
	fixed an out-of-bounds read in the oracle protocol module - its type
		map was one entry shorter than the datatype list it's indexed
		by, so an informix LVARCHAR column read past the end of the
		array and reported whatever followed it as the column type,
		and the index is bounds-checked now as well
	fixed the cs api data reader - seventeen datatype names had no case
		in one or both of its name-to-type chains, so an nvarchar or
		xml column read back as a byte array and a smallfloat column
		read back as bytes while it reported itself as a Single, and
		all 192 datatype names are now handled in both chains, with
		datetimeoffset reading back as a DateTimeOffset
	fixed the cs api data reader - a boolean field was converted with
		Convert.ToBoolean(), which throws on anything but "True" or
		"False", so the 1 informix sends and the t postgresql sends
		were both errors rather than values
	fixed sqlr-listener blaming directory permissions when a unix socket
		name is simply too long - it now says the name is too long,
		how long it is, and what the limit is, and points at the
		instance id and localstatedir, which are what to shorten
	fixed an authentication hole in the des password encryption module -
		it kept one des instance for the life of the process and
		never cleared it, and des appends rather than replaces, so
		each encryption hashed every password the process had seen,
		concatenated - and since des truncates at 8 characters, after
		one successful login with a password of 8 or more characters,
		any password at all authenticated on that connection
	fixed the postgresql_connectstrings auth module - a one-way
		passwordencryption rejected everyone, even on a listener
		configured with passwords="cleartext", where the client sends
		the password itself and a one-way module can check it, and
		the combination that genuinely cannot work, a one-way
		encryption with the md5 challenge/response method, now says
		so in the debug output instead of failing every login
		silently
	documented that a one-way passwordencryption can't be used with a
		challenge/response method, which covers the postgresql
		protocol's default md5 and the firebird protocol's legacy
		hash
	added a firebird_connectstrings auth module, along with a
		sqlrfirebirdcredentials class - it handles both the cleartext
		password that isc_dpb_password carries and the legacy_auth
		des hash that isc_dpb_password_enc carries, and the
		credentials tell it which of the two it was given
	fixed the firebird protocol module - its authenticate() was entirely
		inside an #if 0 and nothing called it, so every user name and
		password was accepted and the password read out of the db
		parameter buffer was discarded - credentials now go through
		the auth framework, and a failed login is answered with the
		same isc_login response a real firebird server sends, rather
		than being let through
	fixed the firebird protocol module - an isc_database_info reply was a
		run of bare item bytes with no lengths and no values, so a
		client read the item bodies that followed one item as its
		length and refused the whole buffer as a malformed clumplet -
		each value is a real cluster now, item byte, 2 byte
		little-endian length and value, an item the module can't
		answer gets isc_info_error rather than a bare byte, seventeen
		items it can answer are answered in the shapes a real
		firebird sends on the wire, the response buffer length the
		client asks for is honored and a reply that would exceed it
		is cut short with isc_info_truncated, and the item walk's
		loop test is bounded rather than comparing pointers for
		inequality
	fixed make install corrupting a running instance - it copied each
		file over the installed one, which keeps the same inode and
		so rewrites the memory-mapped pages of any process that has
		the file open, leaving sqlr-listener and sqlr-connection
		faulting and spinning until killed by hand - each file is
		now written under a temporary name and renamed over the
		installed one, so it gets a new inode and running processes
		keep the file they already have, and an install that fails
		part way leaves the previous version in place rather than
		no file at all
	added per-module install and uninstall targets for the protocol and
		auth modules - src/protocols and src/auths installed their
		base modules from a single group target, so installing one
		module reinstalled all of them, each module now has its own
		installsharedlib, installdll, and uninstall target and the
		group targets just depend on those, which also fixes the
		protocol uninstall target leaving the mysql, postgresql,
		firebird, oracle, tds, and teradata modules behind when built
		with a non-default module name prefix
	fixed the firebird protocol module's attach() accepting any database -
		the path the client sends was read and then never looked at,
		so an attach naming a database that doesn't exist succeeded,
		where a real firebird fails it - the path is now compared,
		after authenticating rather than before, since a real firebird
		checks the credentials first and the file second, against the
		database the instance is configured for, and a mismatch is
		answered with the status vector a real firebird sends for a
		file it can't open - isc_io_error, the failed operation, the
		file name, isc_io_open_err, and the errno text - which also
		meant teaching the status vector builder and the response
		writer to carry string arguments, since neither could before
	fixed the two isDateTimeType overloads disagreeing about
		DATETIMEOFFSET - the one that takes a type name counted it as
		a date/time type and the one that takes a type id did not, so
		the same column was a date/time type or not depending on
		which one the caller happened to use, and a datetimeoffset
		column now binds as a date rather than a string in a bulk
		load, which keeps its offset in the bind's timezone field
		instead of leaving it in an opaque string
	fixed the sap connection module - varbinary columns were reported as
		binary, because ctlib reports both as CS_BINARY_TYPE, and the
		systypes usertype that tells them apart wasn't consulted
	added per-module install and uninstall targets for the rest of the
		modules and executables that installed as a group - the
		directive, errortranslation, filter, logger, pwdenc, query,
		querytranslation, router, and trigger modules, the sqlr-start,
		sqlr-stop, sqlr-listener, sqlr-connection, sqlr-scaler,
		sqlr-cachemanager, and sqlr-status executables, the sqlrsh,
		sqlr-export, sqlr-import, and sqlr-pwdenc executables, and the
		java and perl connection and cursor libraries, so installing
		one reinstalled all of them, each now has its own install and
		uninstall target and the group targets just depend on those,
		which also fixes the pwdenc uninstall target leaving the sha1
		and sha256 modules behind when built with a non-default module
		name prefix
	now requires rudiments 2.3.0 or newer
	fixed the teradata protocol module's decryption of the connect
		message - the first 20 bytes of the encrypted data were being
		skipped as an hmac, but there is no hmac there, the mic is a
		trailer inside the ciphertext instead, so the first block came
		out as garbage and the login parcels were never found
	fixed the teradata protocol module's shared key - the key is a slice
		of the shared secret, chosen by the qop index in the token
		header that the client appends, rather than always the first
		16 bytes
	added mic and token header verification to the teradata protocol
		module's decryption of the connect message
	fixed the teradata protocol module's framing of the encrypted connect
		message - in an encrypted message the lan header stops after
		the session number and the last 28 bytes of it are the first
		bytes of the ciphertext, so decryption started 28 bytes too
		late and the request auth, request number, gateway byte, and
		host character set were read as ciphertext instead of being
		decrypted
	added parsing and debug for the teradata protocol module's logon,
		session option, and sso username request parcels
	documented the license the oracle protocol module carries - it's
		part of the sql relay server, under the gpl version 2 or
		later, and material taken from python-oracledb goes under
		the universal permissive license 1.0 rather than its apache
		2.0 option, which isn't gpl version 2 compatible
	added per-module install and uninstall targets to the directories
		that build a single module - the bindvariabletranslation,
		config, moduledata, notification, parser,
		resultsetheadertranslation, resultsetrowblocktranslation,
		resultsetrowtranslation, resultsettranslation, and schedule
		modules installed straight from a group target with no
		per-module target behind it, so they now follow the same
		pattern as the rest of the tree and there's a block to copy
		when a second module is added to one of them
	removed a stale module rename from the config module install - it
		renamed an installed sqlrconfig_xml module to the platform
		module suffix, but that module was removed years ago, and on
		a platform whose module suffix isn't .so it could rename a
		leftover copy back into a name the server would load
	fixed configure - the chmods and plist fixups that followed AC_OUTPUT
		weren't part of config.status, so regenerating with
		config.status alone left the generated scripts
		non-executable and left ${DESTDIR} in the launchd plists,
		which pointed launchd at a path that doesn't exist - the
		fixups are attached to the files themselves now, so
		config.status carries them, and each of those files can
		also be named individually on the config.status command
		line now
	fixed a dangling pointer in the server's list-api calls - the calls
		that fake a prepare and execute (getCatalogList, getTableList,
		etc.) cleared the bind variable mappings pool without clearing
		the mappings themselves, and the mappings are allocated out of
		that pool, so re-executing the query on that cursor afterward
		read freed memory
	fixed the reformatdatetime result set translation - the dateformat
		and timeformat attributes didn't fall back to datetimeformat,
		as the docs say they do, so with only datetimeformat
		configured, a date-only or time-only field came back to the
		client as an empty string
	fixed the server's date/time reformatting - a field that had no
		format configured for it was replaced with an empty string,
		rather than being left alone
	fixed the postgresql protocol module - none of the character lob
		datatypes had a case in getColumnTypeOid(), so ntext, clob,
		dbclob, longchar, longvarchar, tinytext, mediumtext, longtext,
		json and xml columns were all described to the client as oid
		705, unknown, rather than as text
	fixed the postgresql protocol module - db2's graphic, vargraphic and
		longvargraphic columns were described as oid 705, unknown,
		rather than as text, they're double-byte character types even
		though datatypes.h groups them with the blobs
	fixed the odbc connection module - a string output bind of 4000 bytes
		or more against sql server stored a bind buffer size of 0, so
		subtracting the null terminator from it wrapped to SIZE_MAX,
		which corrupted the heap, returned a truncated value, and
		killed the connection
	fixed the odbc connection module - an empty or zero-length
		input-output string bind wrote its null terminator one byte
		before the start of the bind buffer
	fixed the odbc connection module - the input-output bind conversion
		counted the null terminator twice, so the copy into the bind
		buffer ran two bytes past the end of the converted string
	fixed the odbc connection module's stringSize() - its fallback branch
		didn't count the null terminator, though its other three
		branches did, and its comment said it did
	fixed sqlr-stop - it reported success as soon as it sent SIGTERM,
		without waiting for the process to exit, so processes that
		ignored or were too slow to handle the signal survived a
		successful-looking stop, and their pid files were left behind
		- sqlr-stop now signals every process at once, waits up to 3
		seconds for them to exit, sends SIGKILL to whatever is left,
		waits about another second, and reports success only for the
		processes it saw exit, removing their pid files
	added a nonzero sqlr-stop exit code - it now exits 1 when any process
		is still running after SIGKILL, so init scripts can tell a
		failed stop from a successful one
	fixed sqlr-stop - it stopped the connections before the scaler, whose
		job is to replace connections, so a connection spawned after
		the pid directory had been scanned was never signalled and was
		orphaned when the scaler exited - the scaler is now stopped
		and confirmed dead first, and the sweep for everything else
		runs twice, to catch a connection that hadn't created its pid
		file yet when the first sweep ran
	fixed the sqlr-connection shut down - a connection whose database went
		down while it was running a query would spin at full cpu
		forever, never exiting, after it was sent SIGTERM, because
		reLogIn() logs the connection out and then returns without
		logging back in when the shutdown flag is set, while the loop
		that called it had no shutdown flag check of its own and just
		retried the query against the logged-out connection over and
		over
	fixed sqlr-connection - the handoff path that receives the client file
		descriptor didn't sleep before returning, though its comment
		said it did, so a connection whose listener had died looped
		over the failure at full speed
	fixed the sap connection module - unichar, univarchar and unitext
		columns were all reported as unknown, because ctlib's
		CS_UNICHAR_TYPE and CS_UNITEXT_TYPE had no cases, they report
		nchar, nvarchar and ntext now
	fixed the sap connection module - date, time, bigtime and bigdatetime
		columns were all reported as unknown, because ctlib's
		CS_DATE_TYPE, CS_TIME_TYPE, CS_BIGTIME_TYPE and
		CS_BIGDATETIME_TYPE had no cases, they report date, time, time
		and timestamp now
	fixed the cs api data reader - it compared datatype names
		case-sensitively against the uppercase names in the datatype
		list, but a connection module may report the database's own
		spelling instead, and postgresql's are lowercase, so nothing
		ever matched and every column read back as a byte array, even
		a plain integer one
	documented that the freetds connection's charset connect string
		variable doesn't set the character set that data coming out of
		the database is translated into - the faq had said it did, but
		it sets the character set that freetds asks the server to use,
		which is a different thing, and the one that matters comes
		from the client charset setting in the freetds.conf file
	documented that the sap connection's lang connect string variable
		also selects the character set that data is translated into,
		by way of the locale it names, that charset overrides it, that
		charset takes the client library's own spellings, like utf8
		and iso_1 rather than utf-8 and iso-8859-1, and that an
		unrecognized name fails the login
	fixed the tds protocol module - a failed rpc reported success, its
		done never set the error bit, so the client read the response
		one result out of step and eventually killed the connection
	fixed the tds protocol module - a failed rpc sent 1 as its return
		status where a real sql server sends the error's own number,
		and sent one at all for a procedure that was never found,
		where a real sql server sends none
	fixed the tds protocol module - sp_prepare, sp_prepexec and
		sp_executesql accepted a non-unicode statement or parameter
		declaration argument, a real sql server rejects one with
		error 214
	fixed the tds protocol module - an rpc output parameter's return
		value carried a nullable flag, which made the ct-lib client
		report 65536 rather than 0 for the parameter's user type
	fixed the sap and freetds connection modules - an exec or execute
		query sent everything after the keyword as the rpc procedure
		name, parameters included, and skipped exactly one whitespace
		character after the keyword, so only a bare exec with a single
		space came out right
	fixed the firebird protocol module - readString() and readBuffer()
		allocated a client-supplied 32 bit length with no upper
		bound, so four bytes bought a 4gb allocation before the
		client had authenticated, and readString's length+1 wrapped
		to zero at 0xffffffff and read the body into an empty block;
		the length is now capped at 65535, which is what firebird's
		own api types these lengths as, and rejected before anything
		is allocated
	fixed the firebird protocol module - readString() and readBuffer()
		returned false without freeing the buffer they had just
		allocated, and no caller frees on a false return, so every
		truncated packet leaked whatever the client asked for
	fixed the firebird protocol module - readString() and readBuffer()
		nulled the caller's pointer only after reading the length, so
		a short read of the length left it holding whatever it had
		before; attach() deletes db just before reading it, so a
		truncated attach packet left db dangling and it was deleted a
		second time when the session ended
	fixed the firebird protocol module - readString() and readBuffer()
		printed the length they read with %d, so a hostile 0xffffffff
		logged as -1 rather than the value an operator would be
		looking for
	fixed the odbc connection module - a ms sql server datetime2 column
		was reported as datetime, because the driver reports both with
		the same sql type, length, precision and scale; the column's
		type name is now read at describe time and used to tell them
		apart
	fixed the tds protocol module - preLogin() had no bounds checking at
		all, so a pre-login packet with no terminator walked off the
		end of the request buffer, and an option offset or size was
		used as a copy source without being checked against the packet
		length; a repeated instopt option also leaked its buffer
	fixed the tds protocol module - allHeaders(), rpc() and params()
		each decremented the remaining packet size without first
		checking that there was that much left, so a short request
		underflowed the count to a huge value and the parse ran on
		past the end of the packet
	fixed the odbc connection module - a blob bind couldn't reach a ms
		sql server binary, varbinary or image column at all, because
		blobs were bound as characters there, and sql server won't
		convert a character parameter to a binary one; blobs are now
		bound as binary for sql server
	fixed the odbc connection module - the binary blob bind passed no
		StrLen_or_IndPtr, so the driver looked for a null terminator
		to find the end of the value and truncated any value
		containing a 0 byte, and it ignored the null indicator, so a
		null blob bind bound garbage
	added positioned update, delete and insert to the tds protocol module -
		sp_cursor answered with an error and return status 16957
		before, because a positioned update needs "where current of"
		and the server api has no equivalent; the statement is
		synthesized instead, from the table name the rpc carries, the
		values it names by column, and the primary key values of the
		row the last sp_cursorfetch landed on, which is what an odbc
		driver does for an optimistic cursor; a cursor over a table
		with no primary key, or one that didn't select the key
		columns, is still refused, and so are the operations that
		need a scrollable cursor
	removed the hex workaround from the tds protocol module's bulk load -
		a binary value was sent as hex digits and converted back by
		the insert, because a blob bind couldn't reach sql server;
		the value is bound as a blob now, which halves the bytes sent
		and lifts the varbinary(8000) cap that kept an image column
		from being loaded at its full width
	added the statement lifecycle to the firebird protocol module - a
		firebird client can now connect, prepare, execute and fetch;
		op_detach, op_commit, op_rollback, op_commit_retaining,
		op_rollback_retaining, op_info_transaction,
		op_allocate_statement, op_free_statement, op_set_cursor,
		op_prepare_statement, op_info_sql, op_execute, op_execute2,
		op_exec_immediate, op_exec_immediate2, op_fetch and op_cancel
		all went from a bare "return false;" to real implementations,
		with a sql relay datatype to firebird SQL_* type map, a blr
		message parser and writer, and bind variable handling
	fixed the firebird protocol module's response to an op it doesn't
		implement - sendNotImplementedError() returned false, which
		the session loop reads as "stop looping", so the socket closed
		with no response packet ever written and the client reported a
		lost connection rather than a firebird error
	fixed the firebird protocol module's tpb walk - the loop test was
		exact-equality against the end pointer, so an item whose
		length walked past the end ran the loop off the buffer, and
		the items that carry a length and a value were read as bare
		bytes
	fixed the firebird protocol module's error messages - the message from
		the server api is a buffer with a size, and it was read as a
		string, so an error a client printed ran off the end of the
		text into whatever the buffer still held
	added frb_info_att_charset to what the firebird protocol module
		answers isc_database_info with - isql treats an
		isc_info_error for any item but isc_info_firebird_version as
		proof it's talking to a pre-interbase-6 server and resets its
		sql dialect to 1
	fixed the firebird protocol module's debug output - lengths, handles,
		counts and status vector values are all unsigned, but they
		were printed with %d, so any of them at or above 2147483648
		printed as a negative number - exactly the value an operator
		would be hunting for, misprinted
	removed an unreachable test from the firebird protocol module's
		readPadding() - the degenerate case guard above it already
		returns for the only case in which the padding size can be 0
	fixed the odbc api's column type mapping for the SHORT and TINY
		datatypes - the two comparisons spelled the names the way the
		datatype enum in datatypes.h spells them, "SHORT_DATATYPE"
		and "TINY_DATATYPE", but the string they're compared against
		comes from datatypestring[], which spells them "SHORT" and
		"TINY", so neither branch could ever be taken and a column of
		either type ran off the end of the chain and was reported as
		SQL_CHAR rather than SQL_SMALLINT or SQL_TINYINT
	fixed the jdbc api - it uppercased a column's datatype name with no
		locale before matching it against the names in the datatype
		list, so on a jvm running in a turkish locale "int4"
		uppercased to a dotted capital I followed by NT4 and matched
		nothing, and every postgresql column whose type name had an i
		in it came back from the result set metadata with no type, no
		class name, and the raw column length for its display size and
		precision
	fixed the firebird connection module's date rendering - a DATE column
		was formatted with colons rather than dashes, so 1 January
		2001 rendered as "2001:01:01" instead of "2001-01-01", though
		the date part of a TIMESTAMP rendered correctly
	fixed the firebird connection module's rendering of negative DECIMAL and
		NUMERIC values - the value is a scaled integer, and the parts
		to either side of the decimal point were formatted
		separately, so each printed a sign of its own and -1.50
		rendered as "-1.-50" - and when the integer part was zero it
		had no sign to print at all, so -0.50 rendered as "0.-50"
	fixed the firebird connection module's error messages - the error buffer
		is reused for the life of the connection and is never
		cleared, and the size was measured from the buffer rather
		than from the message just copied into it, so a short error
		following a longer one was reported with the tail of the
		longer one stuck to the end of it, even when the two errors
		belonged to different clients
	capped the response buffer length the firebird protocol module takes
		off the wire - the length a client declares is the ceiling
		that truncates the response, and nothing bounded it, so a
		client that declared a huge one never truncated and the
		response buffer grew to whatever the requested items
		produced - it's capped at 65535 now, which is the ceiling
		firebird's own api implies
	fixed the jdbc api - five result set metadata methods uppercased a
		column's datatype name with no null check, and the name is null
		for an out-of-range column, so asking the metadata about an
		invalid column threw a NullPointerException out of
		getColumnClassName(), getColumnDisplaySize(), getColumnType(),
		getPrecision() or isCurrency() rather than falling through the
		way the rest of the driver does
	fixed the jdbc api's result set metadata for columns whose datatype is
		named SHORT or TINY - two case labels in each of the class name
		and jdbc type chains spelled the identifier in the datatype enum,
		SHORT_DATATYPE and TINY_DATATYPE, rather than the name the server
		sends, so neither could ever match, and with no bare SHORT or TINY
		case to fall back on such a column reported a null class name and
		java.sql.Types.NULL instead of java.lang.Short and Types.SMALLINT
		or Types.TINYINT
	answered more of the firebird protocol module's isc_database_info items -
		it answered 18 of them and sent isc_info_error for the rest, and
		an isc_info_error isn't harmless: isql treats one for any item
		other than isc_info_firebird_version as proof it's talking to a
		pre-interbase-6 server and resets its sql dialect to 1 - the
		page, record and transaction counters, the size and memory
		items, the creation date and the user names are all answered
		now, in the shapes a real server sends them in, and the only
		two items left getting an isc_info_error are
		isc_info_window_turns and isc_info_license, which is what a
		real server sends for those two as well
	fixed two off-by-one reads in the oracle protocol module - the count
		loops in getNullTerminatedArray() and getString() dereferenced
		the read pointer before comparing it to the end of the packet,
		so a packet with no null terminator read one byte past the end
		of the buffer, and if that byte happened to be zero then the
		malformed packet was accepted and the read pointer was left
		past the end of the buffer for the next parse
	fixed the jdbc api - maxFieldSize never truncated a postgresql char
		array or varchar array column, because the two case labels for
		those types spelled the identifiers in the datatype enum rather
		than the CHAR_ARRAY and VARCHAR_ARRAY names the server sends -
		both spellings are accepted now, since with typemangling=lookup
		postgresql sends its own _char and _varchar instead
	fixed an unbounded allocation in the oracle protocol module -
		getAnoArrayField() read an array count off the wire and
		allocated that many uint16_t's without checking it against
		anything, so a count of 0xffffffff asked for 8gb, and since
		nothing in the module catches std::bad_alloc, a failed
		allocation aborted the connection process - the ano parse now
		carries the end of the packet, the field size is checked
		against the bytes left in the packet, the array count is
		checked against the bytes left in the field, and a count that
		doesn't fit fails the negotiation instead of allocating
	added a challenge path to the sqlrauth module api - auth() was verify-
		only, so a protocol whose challenge is derived from the
		password, like oracle's o5logon, couldn't be implemented,
		since only an auth module may see passwords - the new
		challenge() takes the credentials and a buffer, and returns
		true if it built the challenge, and it's exposed on
		sqlrservercontroller for protocol modules to call - the
		protocol module generates the verifier data and passes it in,
		matching what the mysql protocol module already does with its
		nonce, so the auth modules stay stateless - the default
		implementation returns false, so no existing auth module
		changes, and a password stored under a one-way password
		encryption module can't be used, since building the challenge
		requires the cleartext password
	fixed a leak and unbounded reads in the oracle protocol module's
		authentication request parse - recvAuthenticationRequest()
		duplicated the user name, and a name and a value per field,
		and never freed any of them, and the client chooses how many
		fields there are, so one request could leak thousands of
		blocks in a long-lived connection process - the strings are
		freed now, the field loop is a while (rp<end) rather than a
		do/while so a packet with no fields parses none instead of
		one, and the header, the user name and every read in the loop
		are checked against the end of the packet
	fixed the jdbc api - result set metadata getColumnType() returned
		java.sql.Types.NULL, which means "the sql NULL type", for a
		datatype name it has no case for, rather than Types.OTHER,
		which is what jdbc defines for a type the driver cannot
		classify - and postgresql reports either its own type names
		or bare oids depending on typemangling, so in the default
		configuration every column of every postgresql result set
		came back as Types.NULL
	rewrote the oracle userlist auth module, which was a copy of the
		mysql userlist module with the names changed - its
		oracle_native_password method computed mysql's sha1-xor
		scramble, which oracle doesn't do at any version, and its
		supported method list still named mysql's
		authentication_windows_client - it implements oracle's real
		o5logon verifier exchange now, for both the 11g and the 12c
		verifier types, using the challenge path that was just added
		to the auth modules, and the method is called o5logon rather
		than oracle_native_password
	fixed the oracle userlist auth module - a password stored under a
		one-way password encryption module never authenticated,
		because the module only ever tried to decrypt the configured
		password, which a one-way module can't do - it encrypts the
		password that was passed in and compares now, the way the
		sqlrclient userlist module already did, for the
		oracle_clear_password method - one-way encryption still can't
		work with o5logon, which derives its challenge from the
		password and so needs the cleartext
	fixed the oracle userlist auth module - it never set the password
		path on the value parser, so a password written in the
		configuration as an indirect value, such as a file reference,
		had no base path to resolve against, although the same
		password did in the mysql userlist module
	fixed the firebird connection module - a decimal or numeric column's
		scale divisor was computed as (int)pow(10.0,scale), and
		firebird allows 18 digits of scale, so from scale 10 up the
		divisor overflowed an int and every value of magnitude
		2147483648 or more rendered as garbage - decimal(18,10)
		holding 1.5 came back as 6.2115098112 - the divisor is now
		accumulated by integer multiplication into a 64-bit integer,
		which is exact at every scale firebird allows and also drops
		the floating point round trip that could otherwise give a
		divisor one less than a power of ten
	made the firebird protocol module honor a read-only transaction - a tpb
		asking for one got a read-write transaction, because SQL Relay
		has no way to ask a backend for a read-only one, so a write
		inside it succeeded when it should have failed - an insert,
		update or delete is refused by the module now, with the bare
		isc_read_only_trans a real server sends, which the client turns
		into sqlcode -817, sqlstate 42000 and "attempted update during
		read-only transaction" - isc_info_tra_access answers read-only
		for such a transaction too, rather than always answering
		read-write - what it still doesn't do: the transaction
		underneath is read-write (#9109), ddl isn't refused (#9107), and
		the tpb's isolation level is still dropped (#9108)
	fixed the oracle protocol module - the protocol negotiation response
		was incomplete, it never sent the server compile-time or
		runtime capability arrays at all, and it hardcoded the
		database and national character sets to us7ascii - the
		response now carries capability arrays captured from a live
		oracle 11.2 server, defaults to al32utf8 and al16utf16, and
		takes charset and nationalcharset module parameters
	fixed the jdbc api - the result set metadata class never validated
		the column number, so all twenty of its accessors that take
		one answered for a column that doesn't exist rather than
		throwing - and answered plausibly, with isSearchable(),
		isSigned() and isWritable() reporting true, isNullable()
		reporting columnNoNulls, and eight others returning exactly
		what a real column returns - they all throw an "invalid
		column index" SQLException now, the way the result set class
		already did
	fixed the oracle protocol module - the ano request header and six of
		the fixed-size ano field getters had no bounds checks, so a
		short packet read past the end of the buffer, before
		authentication - an 8-byte data packet left a zero-byte buffer
		to read a 15-byte header out of - the header, the service
		count and every fixed-size field are bounded against the end
		of the packet now, and getAnoUnknownField(), which was dead
		code that advanced the read pointer by a length taken off the
		wire, is gone
	fixed the jdbc api - result set findColumn() and the by-label
		accessors called equalsIgnoreCase() on column names without
		checking for null, and the names are null when the server
		was told not to send column info, which an application can
		do through unwrap().dontGetColumnInfo() - the column count
		still comes back non-zero in that state, so both threw a
		NullPointerException rather than the SQLException jdbc
		requires
	fixed the oracle protocol module - the ano negotiation ran on every
		connection, but ano is optional, and oracle's own thin drivers
		don't negotiate it - python-oracledb and node-oracledb send the
		protocol negotiation as their first data packet, which the
		module read as an ano request, failed on the missing 0xdeadbeef
		marker, and dropped the connection - the connect packet's nsi
		flags say whether the client wants ano, and the negotiation
		only runs when they do
	fixed the firebird protocol module - a debug line printed an ssize_t
		with %d, which reads 4 bytes of an 8 byte argument, so the
		byte count it showed dropped the high half on a little-endian
		machine and was wrong outright on a big-endian one - it prints
		with %lld and an explicit 64 bit cast now, which is what the
		rest of the tree already does with the same value
	fixed the firebird connection module - getError() left the error buffer
		unterminated when the message was as long as the buffer or
		longer, because it clamped the size to the buffer size, filled
		the buffer to the brim, and then correctly declined to write a
		terminator one byte past the end - a truncated message was
		therefore not a c string, and the readers that walk it to a
		terminator, such as the one that hands it to printf as a format
		string, read past it - it clamps to one less than the buffer
		size now and always terminates, so a byte of the buffer holds
		the terminator rather than the byte past it, which isn't the
		module's to write
	the oracle protocol module now warns when a client asks for oracle
		native network encryption or crypto-checksumming, which it
		declines - the offered algorithm lists were read and thrown away
		unread, so the decline was silent and an administrator whose
		client failed got nothing from the server side - the module also
		reads those lists correctly now, as one byte per algorithm id,
		which is what the encryption and crypto-checksumming services
		actually send - only SQLNET.ENCRYPTION_CLIENT=REQUIRED or
		SQLNET.CRYPTO_CHECKSUM_CLIENT=REQUIRED fails, with ORA-12660,
		and the limitation is documented in the config guide
	fixed the firebird protocol module - a response buffer length that
		arrived sign-extended from a 16 bit field was folded back down at
		two of the five requests that carry one and not at the other
		three, so a client that asked for 32768 bytes got a 65535 byte
		ceiling at three of them - firebird folds it wherever the field
		appears, and now so does this, in the same helper that caps it
	fixed the firebird protocol module - the response buffer length on an
		op_exec_immediate2 request was kept in the session, but that
		request's reply is an sql response rather than an info response, so
		the length governs nothing - firebird's own server ignores it and
		its client sends zero - it's read and dropped now
	documented the oracle protocol module's charset and nationalcharset
		listener attributes in the config guide - what each one
		advertises, the al32utf8 and al16utf16 defaults, that the module
		advertises the character set ids rather than converting anything,
		so they have to match what the back-end actually returns, and
		that 178, we8mswin1252, is the fallback for a client that can't
		decode a result set, since every byte decodes under cp1252
	fixed the oracle protocol module - it only ever answered tti
		protocol version 5, which no modern client can use - ojdbc
		requires the server capability arrays, which only exist at
		version 6, and python-oracledb offers version 6 alone, so
		the module dropped its connection without answering - the
		module now selects the highest version the client offers
		that it implements, up to 6, sends the capability arrays
		only at version 6, where a real server sends them, and
		answers connect protocol version 11 (314) rather than 8
		(310)
	fixed the tds protocol module - nothing recorded whether a session had
		logged in and nothing checked, so a client could send a sql
		batch, bulk load or rpc as its very first request and have it
		run, as whatever user the connection was configured for - the
		listener doesn't authenticate, every protocol module owns
		that, so there was nothing else in the way - the session
		tracks its login now, and rejects a request that doesn't
		belong in its state, in both directions, since a login
		arriving a second time would re-auth the session under the
		modules and renegotiate the wire format with the previous
		user's cursors still open
	fixed the firebird protocol module - a client that prepared a statement
		and asked what its bind variables were got told every one of them
		was a nullable string 4000 bytes wide, so a client that filled a
		typed buffer from the answer sent the wrong bytes and the insert
		failed - for an insert the module now works the types out from the
		columns the binds feed, which is the same answer the backend would
		give, and anything it can't resolve still describes as a string
	fixed the tds protocol module - tds7Login() read 86 bytes of fixed
		header, or 94 from tds 7.2 up, without ever checking that the
		packet was that long, so a truncated login read past the end
		of the buffer before any of the offset arithmetic began - the
		request size is carried into the function now and checked
		before the header reads, and again inside the 7.2 branch,
		whose extra 8 bytes can't be checked with the rest because
		the version that selects them comes off the wire
	fixed the tds protocol module - a login field that exceeded its
		maximum was dropped, but its length was kept, and the null
		pointer and the nonzero length then travelled downstream
		together - a language field longer than 128 characters killed
		the connection in the environment-change response, and with
		debug on an extension field longer than 255 bytes killed it
		before the client had authenticated - the length is dropped
		along with the field now
	fixed the tds protocol module - readPassword() computed its byte
		count into a 16 bit variable, from a character count that
		nothing in the function bounded, so a large count truncated
		and the decode read past the copy - it bounds the count
		itself now and computes in a size_t
	fixed the oracle protocol module - it authenticated nobody and
		refused nobody: the session key it sent was a hardcoded
		literal and cont->auth() was never called, so any password
		was accepted - it runs the real o5logon exchange now, with
		generated verifier data, a session key from an auth module,
		AUTH_SVR_RESPONSE, and a real ORA-01017 or ORA-01005 on
		failure, and the request parser was rewritten on the real
		wire layout
	added a verifiertype attribute to the oracle protocol module's
		listener tag, 11g or 12c, defaulting to 12c
	fixed the oracle protocol module - an unknown user was refused a
		round trip earlier than a wrong password, and with a
		different error, so a client could tell which user names
		exist - it fabricates a challenge for a user no auth module
		knows now, runs the whole exchange, and answers ORA-01017,
		which is what a real server does
	fixed the oracle protocol module - it refused every modern client's
		datatype negotiation request: the two 16 bit values after
		the ttc code are the client's character sets, in host byte
		order, not the markers an 8.0.5 client happened to make
		them look like, and the byte after them is a bit field of
		encoding flags rather than a third marker - all three are
		read as what they are now
	fixed the tds protocol module - the login's sspi block was the one
		field with no maximum, and its length can be 32 bits, so a
		client asking for the whole range got a 4 gigabyte allocation
		followed by a 4 gigabyte read out of a packet a few hundred
		bytes long - the allocation succeeded and the copy then took
		the connection down - it's bounded now, and bounded against
		the packet as well, which also stops a tds 7.0 client asking
		for 64k it never sent
	fixed the tds protocol module - nothing checked that a login field's
		offset was inside the packet, only that its length was within
		its maximum, so an offset near the top of its 16 bit range
		read up to 64k past the request, and whatever it found became
		a user name, a database name or a line in the debug log -
		every field's offset is checked now, sspi included, and a
		field that doesn't fit is dropped the same way an over-long
		one is
	fixed the sap and freetds connection modules - a lang= the client
		library doesn't recognize fails the login inside
		cs_ctx_alloc, which is before the message callback exists, so
		the library's own explanation went to stderr and all the log
		said was that a context structure couldn't be allocated,
		which reads like a memory or client library problem rather
		than a typo in the connect string - the error names the
		locale now, and says the details went to stderr
	fixed the sap and freetds connection modules - a login error the
		client library had no text for ended in a dangling colon
	implemented data type negotiation in the oracle protocol module -
		it echoed the client's request back, capability arrays and
		all, which no client can read - it sends the data types a
		real oracle 11.2 server sends now, and the db time zone,
		time zone version and national character set that an oci
		client asks for, and it reads the rest of the request:
		both client capability arrays, the negotiated field
		version, and the client's own type list
	fixed the sap connection module - a char, varchar, nchar or nvarchar
		column reported LONGCHAR, and a binary or varbinary column
		reported LONGBINARY, whenever the column was wider than 255
		bytes, or whenever charset= was one that expands - the client
		library reports those as its long types, and the module read
		only the type, not the systypes usertype that comes with it
		and says what the column really is - so charset=utf8, which
		is what the documentation tells people to set, turned every
		character column into a clob to jdbc and into varbinary to a
		tds client - the long arms split on the usertype now, the
		same way the short ones already did
	fixed the firebird protocol module - it ran a select at prepare time
		to find out what columns the result set had, even when the
		backend had just told it, so a client that prepared a select
		and never executed it ran the query anyway and the backend's
		cursor opened too early - it only runs the select now when
		the columns aren't already known
	fixed the tds protocol module - the list of packet types a client is
		allowed to send included the one type only a server sends, so
		the list said something untrue about the protocol - a client
		that sent it got no further either way, but the next person
		reasoning about which types can reach a parser had to work
		out for themselves that it was a mistake
	fixed the oracle protocol module - it cleared two capability bits
		that its own footers depend on, so a client that logged in
		successfully hung forever and one that got the module's
		ORA-01017 saw a client-side exception instead - the footers
		are captures of an 11.2 server that sets both bits, so the
		module sets them too now, and a modern client can log in
	fixed the odbc connection module - a null bind couldn't reach a sql
		server binary or varbinary column at all, because the client
		api flattens a null blob bind into a plain null bind and the
		null bind is generic - one call serves every bind type and
		every column type, and it bound a character parameter, which
		sql server won't implicitly convert to binary - it asks the
		driver what the parameter is aimed at now, for null binds
		only, and binds binary only when the driver says binary, so
		a parameter the driver can't describe keeps the character
		bind it has always had
	fixed the odbc connection module - smalldatetime, money and smallmoney
		were reported as datetime and decimal, because sql server
		reports each pair the same way and only the type name tells
		them apart - they're distinguished by name now, the way
		datetime2 already was
	fixed the tds protocol module - it described smalldatetime, real,
		money, smallmoney and uniqueidentifier columns wrongly on an
		ordinary select: the narrow types got the wide type's width
		because the only thing separating them is the column's type
		and the module was going by the column's size, which is a
		digit count on some back ends and a printed width on others -
		money and smallmoney also carried a precision and scale that
		a real server doesn't send, and a uniqueidentifier was
		described as 36 bytes, the width of its printed form, rather
		than the 16 it actually occupies
	fixed the tds protocol module - it wrote a money value by removing the
		decimal point and multiplying by a fixed 100, which is only
		right when the back end renders exactly two decimal places -
		mssql renders four, so the value went out a hundred times too
		large - the fraction is padded or truncated to the four
		places the protocol wants now, whatever the back end sent
	documented when the odbc connection module's mars option is needed -
		sql relay gives each cursor its own statement handle on one
		connection, and ms sql server's odbc driver only allows one
		statement to have results pending unless mars is on, so any
		instance running more than one cursor at a time against it
		wants mars=yes
	fixed the oracle protocol module's query3 request parse - the sequence
		number after the tti function code was never consumed, the
		options word was read as two big endian shorts rather than
		one ub4, and the query size was read as a little endian int
		at a guessed offset, so a modern client's query size came out
		as -252 and no query ran at all
	rewrote the oracle protocol module's query3 and fetch responses - a
		describe, a row header, the rows, a return parameters block
		and a summary object, built field by field in the shape a
		live oracle 11.2 server sends, in place of the 8i era byte
		strings and the bare ok footer that a modern client can't
		parse, with rows sent for the prefetch count the request asks
		for rather than only when the fetch option is set, a fetch
		request parser so a result set bigger than one prefetch
		works, and an oracle number encoder so a numeric column can
		be returned at all
	changed the oracle protocol module's AUTH_VERSION_NO from 8.0.5 to
		11.2.0.1.0, which is what its capability array, data type
		table and field version already answer as - ojdbc picks its
		result set reader from that number, and told 8.0.5 it read an
		11.2 shaped describe with an 8.0 era reader
	fixed the oracle protocol module's ano debug output - the encryption
		service opened its block with a plain write rather than a
		start, so it closed a block it never opened and everything
		after it, including the whole ano response, was indented one
		level too far left
	fixed the tds protocol module - a decimal or numeric rpc parameter
		carried a one byte length that nothing checked, and the
		value was read into a 16 byte array on the stack, so a
		client could write up to 254 bytes of its own choosing over
		that array and over the saved registers and return address
		behind it - a length of 0 was worse still, because the
		length minus one became -1 and then a copy of the whole
		address space - the length is bounded now, and 0 is read as
		the null the protocol says it is
	added a debug dump of an ano array field the oracle protocol module
		can't recognize, so the shape gets captured rather than
		silently ignored
	fixed the oracle protocol module's refuse packet - it carried no body
		at all, so a client that couldn't agree a protocol version
		got a refusal with no reason in it, and it now carries the
		two reason bytes, the length and the connect-string-shaped
		message a real listener sends, with ORA-12564 in it, which
		ojdbc reports as "TNS:connection refused"
	fixed the oracle protocol module's tti version negotiation - it
		dropped the connection without answering when it couldn't
		agree a version, and it now sends an ORA-03134 error packet,
		which is what a server can send after the accept - a refuse
		packet is only valid before it
	removed the oracle protocol module's unimplemented redirect packet,
		which had no caller and whose body is a connect string
		pointing at another listener, which sql relay has no use
		for
	fixed the oracle protocol module - it read the tns data flags in front
		of every tti message, but they belong to the packet, so a
		packet carrying more than one message desynced, and a client
		that packs a close-cursors piggyback in front of its logoff
		got no answer to the logoff and reported ORA-03113
	decoded the oracle protocol module's close-cursors piggyback, which
		was read as a fixed 11 bytes with no bounds check, and it now
		reads the sequence number, pointer, cursor count and cursor
		ids, and releases each cursor named
	fixed the oracle protocol module's logoff response - it sent a
		truncated status message, and it now sends the call status
		and end-to-end sequence number a real server sends, so a
		client's disconnect completes cleanly
	the firebird connection module describes a query's result set when the
		query is prepared now, rather than when it runs, and reports
		its column info as valid after the prepare - so a select's
		columns are known without running it, which is what the
		firebird protocol module needs to answer op_prepare_statement
		for a select with bind variables
	fixed a memory leak in the firebird connection module - the per-column
		text buffers were never freed, so a connect string setting
		maxcolumncount=0 leaked one maxfieldsize+1 byte buffer per
		column per query
	fixed a crash in the firebird connection module - with maxcolumncount=0
		in the connect string, re-executing a select walked a null
		field array, because closing the result set freed the buffers
		and the describe then sized itself from a column count that
		was already gone - the buffers are kept across a close now,
		and the describe grows them itself, which also stops the
		column names the server caches from dangling
	added the blob operations to the firebird protocol module - a client
		can create a blob, fill it with segments, close it, bind its id
		into an insert, select it back, open it, read it, seek in it,
		and ask isc_blob_info how long it is, where every one of those
		answered "feature is not supported" and dropped the connection
		before
	added the second wire encoding for the oracle protocol module's
		authentication exchange - a client whose platform matches the
		one the module names in its tti protocol response marshals the
		request in its own memory layout, with 8 byte pointer
		sentinels, little endian counts and a length prefixed user
		name, rather than portably, and an oci client got ORA-03113 at
		the first authentication packet because of it

